1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { In this chapter we will focus on early gambits starting from this position. } 3. d4 { This position is covered in the chapters about the Scotch, but here we will have a look at some Gambit tries. } exd4 4. c3 { A Goring Gambit. -KEY- } d5 $5 { A simple way to avoid any gambits and achieve a comfortable position. 4...dxc3 5. Bc4 cxb2 6.Bxb2 is really interesting but it's not practical to go into something so complex when we don't have to. } 5. exd5 Qxd5 6. cxd4 Bg4 { We have a very easy development. } 7. Be2 { 7.Nc3 Bb4 8.Be2 transposes. } Bb4+ 8. Nc3 Bxf3 { Very concrete, we have 9...Qc4 in mind. The idea is that in the endgame we will play against the isolated d-pawn. } 9. Bxf3 Qc4 { The queen is very annoying on c4 stopping White from castling. } 10. Qb3 Qxb3 11. axb3 Nge7 $11 { with long castle next and no problems. This was the course of the game R.Forster-M.Godena which you may want to look at. } 
