[Result "*"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { In this chapter we will focus on early gambits starting from this position. } 3. d4 { This position is covered in the chapters about the Scotch, but here we will have a look at some Gambit tries. } exd4 4. Bc4 { This is called a Scotch Gambit. -KEY- } Bc5 { 4...Nf6 is possible but that would trick us into a line of the Italian which is out of the scope of this repertoire. } 5. Ng5 $1 { A very direct attempt and much better than 5. O-O. } Nh6 6. Nxf7 ( { or } 6. Bxf7 { but not } 6. Qh5 Qf6 { and White didn't manage to deprive us from castling rights. } ) 
Nxf7 7. Bxf7+ Kxf7 8. Qh5+ { With this trick White regains the sacrificed piece. } g6 9. Qxc5 d5 $1 { Our king is a bit weak but we have a lead in the development so we try to open the game. } 10. O-O ( 10. Qxd5 { is just bad. } Qxd5 11. exd5 Nb4 { with a big advantage. } ) 
dxe4 11. c3 ( { White needs to open the game somehow. } 11. Bf4 { stops ...Qd6 but we have } Qe7 ) 
Qd6 { Not the only move but let's try to exchange queens so that weakness of our king won't be felt. } 12. Qxd6 ( 12. Qb5 a6 { , probably there are no good squares for White's queen. } ) 
cxd6 13. cxd4 Nxd4 14. Nc3 { White is currently pawn down but things are really tricky and we shouldn't try to hold on to that extra pawn. } Bg4 $1 ( { Playing for the activity. Note that surprisingly after } 14... Re8 15. Be3 { Black is busted. For instance } Nc2 16. Rac1 Nxe3 17. fxe3 Kg7 18. Nd5 { White is dominating and objectively winning. } ) 
15. Nxe4 Nc2 ( { Black is about to win the exchange as } 15... Nc2 15. Rb1 { is met by } Be2 { - White can get a pawn on d6 in return and objectively it might be equal but probably it is White who needs to prove it. } ) 

 *

