1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { In this chapter we will focus on early gambits starting from this position. } 3. Nc3 Nf6 { This is the Four Knights but here we will look at the Belgrade and Halloween Gambits. } 4. d4 exd4 5. Nd5 { The Belgrade Gambit, which is one of the weirdest opening ideas I have seen- what are the benefits of plonking the knight on d5? I found a great antidote to this line. -KEY- } Nb4 $1 { A very concrete reply, which promises Black a slight edge in many lines. } 6. Bc4 $6 { Everything gets semi forced for many moves now. } Nbxd5 7. exd5 Bb4+ 8. Bd2 Qe7+ 9. Qe2 Bxd2+ 10. Kxd2 Qxe2+ 11. Kxe2 c5 { A nice way of forcing 12.dxc6 as otherwise White's structure would be bad (weak d5 pawn). } 12. dxc6 bxc6 13. Nxd4 d5 { Not much to explain. Such moves are super natural and obvious. } 14. Bd3 c5 15. Nb5 Kd7 $15 { Black will put the king on c6 and his hanging pawns will rather be a strength than a weakness. } 
