1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { In this chapter we will focus on early gambits starting from this position. } 3. Nc3 Nf6 { This is the Four Knights but here we will look at the Belgrade and Halloween Gambits. } 4. Nxe5 $2 { The so-called Halloween Gambit, which is hardly an opening suited for a serious game. The sacrifice is obviously unsound and Black should be winning quite easily. -KEY- } Nxe5 5. d4 Ng6 6. e5 Ng8 { It looks ugly but we don't want to give our piece back. We only suffer for one move which is a low price for a piece. } 7. Bc4 d5 $1 { Sacrificing a pawn to fasten the piece development. } 8. Bxd5 N8e7 9. Bg5 { White also develops fast but he is far from creating a real threat. } ( 9. Bb3 { is met by } Nf5 $1 ) 
c6 10. Bb3 h6 11. Qf3 { This may look scary but fortunately it just doesn't work. } hxg5 ( { If you are scared to calculate any lines you can play } 11... Be6 { and it's still winning for Black. } ) 
12. Qxf7+ Kd7 13. d5 Kc7 { Our king is safe. White can regain one piece but it simplifies the position further. } 14. d6+ Kb8 15. dxe7 Nxe7 { With a completely winning position. } 
