1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { In this chapter we will focus on early gambits starting from this position. } 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. O-O Nf6 5. d4 ( { An attempt to transpose into the Max Lange Attack after } 5. d4 exd4 6. e5 { . However, Black can simply capture the pawn with the bishop, achieving a better game. } ) 
 { -KEY- } Bxd4 6. Nxd4 Nxd4 7. f4 $6 { That's the most popular, but probably not so good. } d6 8. fxe5 dxe5 9. Bg5 { White's play may be appealing at first, but the compensation is not enough for a full pawn. } Qe7 { Black threatens ...Qc5 and prepares a long castle. } ( { Less accurate is } 9... Qd6 { because of } 10. c3 { and exchange of queens. } ) 
 ( { We should also mention the game Movessian-Adams, Wijk aan Zee 2009 where Black played logical } 9... Be6 { , but after } 10. Na3 Qe7 11. c3 { White obtained dangerous initiative as Black had to go back to c6. Our move order is more flexible, we may play ...Be6 although most likely we will choose ...Bd7 and for now it's important to have ...Ne6 if needed. } ) 
10. c3 $2 { Forcing the issues is counterproductive. } Ne6 { Hitting the bishop on g5. As mentioned earlier, this is an important resource not wasting any time in a position where dynamic factors matters a lot. } 11. Bxf6 gxf6 12. Na3 Nf4 $1 { a great place for our knight. } 13. Kh1 h5 $19 { Preparing 14...h4, cementing the knight on f4. Then Black can follow with ...Bd7 and ...0-0-0. White not only doesn't have a compensation but his position is miserable. } 
